<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Start With a Pantry Zone Map</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Before you buy a single container, decide what each shelf is for. The rule is the same one we use in <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/bathroom-organization-ideas/">bathroom organization</a>: the more often you reach for something, the easier it should be to reach.</p>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Eye level</strong> is prime space. Keep the food you use every day here: breakfast, pasta, rice, oils and your own snacks.</li>



<li><strong>Waist level</strong> is for cooking and baking supplies: canned goods, grains, jars and a baking bin.</li>



<li><strong>Low shelves</strong> hold heavy items and anything kids should reach on their own.</li>



<li><strong>The top shelf</strong> is for backups and things you use a few times a year.</li>



<li><strong>The door</strong> is for small, flat items like spices and seasoning packets.</li>
</ul>



